From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:38:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: do not block on incoming-call delivery (legacy IMS
 deadlocks the main thread)

ImsPhoneCallTracker.onIncomingCall defaults to executeAndWait(), i.e.
CompletableFuture.runAsync(task, mExecutor).join(), and the production constructor injects
phone.getContext().getMainExecutor(). A modern ImsService calls onIncomingCall on a binder thread,
where blocking costs nothing -- hence ro.telephony.block_binder_thread_on_incoming_calls. The pre-P
compat layer delivers it from MmTelFeatureCompatAdapter's ACTION_IMS_INCOMING_CALL broadcast
receiver, which runs on the main thread, so join() waits for a task that only the waiting thread can
run. Self-deadlock.

Everything after that is downstream. PhoneInterfaceManager.sendRequest posts to the main thread and
waits with no timeout, so every unrelated telephony call hangs too: that is why Settings ANRed on
isVoNrEnabled, and why several binder threads were parked on MainThreadRequest monitors. Eventually
TelephonyConnectionService could not execute, the phone process was killed for ANR, and the next
call failed with 'Phone is null, OUT_OF_SERVICE'.

Confirmed from the ANR trace: com.android.phone main thread in CompletableFuture.join under
executeAndWait <- onIncomingCall <- notifyIncomingCallSession <- MmTelFeatureCompatAdapter$2.onReceive.

AOSP supplies the switch and its else branch names the case: 'for legacy IMS we want to avoid
blocking the binder thread'.
---
 device.mk | 18 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+)

diff --git a/device.mk b/device.mk
index be7e1db..631c90f 100644
--- a/device.mk
+++ b/device.mk
@@ -377,6 +377,24 @@ PRODUCT_PACKAGES += ImsBridge
 # VT natives can dlopen and ImsVideoGlobals.init() can run. See BoardConfig.mk TARGET_LD_SHIM_LIBS.
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += libshim_vtsurface
 
+# Incoming-call delivery must not block, because on the compat path it blocks the MAIN thread.
+#
+# ImsPhoneCallTracker.onIncomingCall defaults to executeAndWait(), which is
+# CompletableFuture.runAsync(task, mExecutor).join(), and mExecutor is
+# phone.getContext().getMainExecutor(). A modern ImsService calls onIncomingCall on a binder thread,
+# so blocking it is harmless -- hence the property name. The pre-P compat layer instead delivers it
+# from MmTelFeatureCompatAdapter's ACTION_IMS_INCOMING_CALL broadcast receiver, which runs on the
+# main thread, so join() waits for a task that can only run on the thread doing the waiting.
+#
+# com.android.phone's main thread then never returns. Every PhoneInterfaceManager.sendRequest posts
+# to that thread and waits without a timeout, so unrelated telephony calls hang too -- that is what
+# made Settings ANR on isVoNrEnabled -- and eventually TelephonyConnectionService cannot execute,
+# the phone process is killed for ANR, and the next call fails with "Phone is null, OUT_OF_SERVICE".
+#
+# AOSP provides exactly this switch, and its else-branch comment names the case: "for legacy IMS we
+# want to avoid blocking the binder thread".
+PRODUCT_PROPERTY_OVERRIDES += ro.telephony.block_binder_thread_on_incoming_calls=false
+
 # nanopb 0.2.8, for libril-qc-qmi-1.so alone. See nanopb-0.2.8/Android.bp for why a second copy of
 # nanopb has to exist at all.
 PRODUCT_PACKAGES += libnanopb_legacy
